<!--
	Examples for CSS Color 4 § 12 Comparing <color> Values.

	These are the worked pairs from the "Are these two colors equivalent?" app,
	<https://apps.colorjs.io/equivalent-colors/>, which implements the § 12 algorithm
	step by step. Each example below can be pasted into the section as-is; they are
	grouped by which step of the algorithm decides the answer, and each one is chosen
	to exercise a different clause.

	These have since been merged into CSS Color 4 § 12; this file is kept as the
	working copy the app's examples were derived from.
-->

	<div class="example" id="ex-equivalent-same-space">
		<span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(100% 0% 0%)"></span>&nbsp;''red''
		and <span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(100% 0% 0%)"></span>&nbsp;''rgb(255, 0, 0)''
		are [=equivalent colors=].
		Both are in the ''srgb'' <<color-space>>,
		so step 2 compares their red, green, blue
		and alpha components, which are all equal.
	</div>

	<div class="example" id="ex-equivalent-srgb-function">
		<span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(100% 0% 0%)"></span>&nbsp;''red''
		and <span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(100% 0% 0%)"></span>&nbsp;''color(srgb 1 0 0)''
		are [=equivalent colors=].
		A [=named color=] is considered to be in the ''srgb'' <<color-space>>,
		which is also the <<color-space>> of this ''color()'' value,
		so the two are compared component by component at step 2.
	</div>

	<div class="example" id="ex-equivalent-alpha">
		<span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(100% 0% 0% / 0.502)"></span>&nbsp;''#ff000080''
		and <span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(100% 0% 0% / 0.5)"></span>&nbsp;''rgb(255 0 0 / 50%)''
		are <em>not</em> [=equivalent colors=],
		although they are hard to tell apart.
		The alpha channel is compared like any other component,
		and 128 / 255 = 0.50196…, which differs from 0.5
		by more than a small ε.
	</div>

	<div class="example" id="ex-equivalent-powerless-hsl">
		<span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(50% 50% 50%)"></span>&nbsp;''hsl(0 0% 50%)''
		and <span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(50% 50% 50%)"></span>&nbsp;''hsl(200 0% 50%)''
		are [=equivalent colors=],
		by either of two routes.
		Their saturation is 0%, so at step 1
		the hue of each becomes a [=missing component=],
		and a [=missing component=] is equal to another [=missing component=].
		Independently, ''hsl()'' is considered to be in the ''srgb'' <<color-space>>,
		where the hue is not one of the components being compared at all.
	</div>

	<div class="example" id="ex-equivalent-powerless-hwb">
		<span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(100% 100% 100%)"></span>&nbsp;''white''
		and <span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(100% 100% 100%)"></span>&nbsp;''hwb(120 100% 0%)''
		are [=equivalent colors=].
		Whiteness and blackness sum to 100,
		so the hue is [=powerless=] and becomes [=missing=] at step 1;
		and since ''hwb()'' is considered to be in the ''srgb'' <<color-space>>,
		both colors are compared as R, G and B of 100%.
	</div>

	<div class="example" id="ex-equivalent-powerless-oklch">
		<span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(38.8573% 38.8573% 38.8573%)"></span>&nbsp;''oklch(50% 0 40)''
		and <span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(38.8573% 38.8573% 38.8573%)"></span>&nbsp;''oklch(50% 0 200)''
		are [=equivalent colors=].
		The chroma is zero, so each hue is [=powerless=]
		and becomes [=missing=] at step 1,
		leaving two colors whose every component compares as equal.
	</div>

	<div class="example" id="ex-equivalent-powerless-epsilon">
		<span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(46.6334% 46.6325% 46.6323%)"></span>&nbsp;''lch(50% 0.001 30)''
		and <span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(46.6319% 46.6329% 46.6329%)"></span>&nbsp;''lch(50% 0.001 200)''
		are [=equivalent colors=],
		even though their chroma is not exactly zero
		and their hues are 170 degrees apart.
		A chroma of 0.001 is within the ε of 0.0015
		that ''lch()'' defines for a [=powerless=] hue,
		so at step 1 both hues become [=missing=]
		and both chromas are set to zero.
	</div>

	<div class="example" id="ex-equivalent-hue-normalization">
		<span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(64.9582% 38.5878% 47.1578%)"></span>&nbsp;''lch(50% 30 0)''
		and <span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(64.9582% 38.5878% 47.1578%)"></span>&nbsp;''lch(50% 30 360)''
		are [=equivalent colors=],
		but not because of anything this algorithm does:
		a <<hue>> is normalized to the range [0, 360) when it is parsed,
		so by the time the two are compared
		both hue components are 0.
	</div>

	<div class="example" id="ex-equivalent-missing-vs-zero">
		<span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(0% 50.1961% 0%)"></span>&nbsp;''rgb(none 128 0)''
		and <span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(0% 50.1961% 0%)"></span>&nbsp;''rgb(0 128 0)''
		are <em>not</em> [=equivalent colors=],
		although they render identically,
		since a [=missing component=] behaves as zero when a color is displayed.
		For the purposes of this comparison a [=missing component=]
		is only ever equal to another [=missing component=],
		never to a component whose value happens to be zero.
	</div>

	<div class="example" id="ex-equivalent-missing-cross-space">
		<span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(38.8573% 38.8573% 38.8573%)"></span>&nbsp;''oklch(50% 0 none)''
		and <span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(38.8573% 38.8573% 38.8573%)"></span>&nbsp;''oklab(50% 0 0)''
		are <em>not</em> [=equivalent colors=],
		despite being colorimetrically identical.
		They are in different <<color-space>>s,
		and the first has a [=missing component=],
		so step 3 returns false
		without any component values being compared.
	</div>

	<div class="example" id="ex-equivalent-oklab">
		<span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(100% 0% 0%)"></span>&nbsp;''red''
		and <span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(100% 0% 0%)"></span>&nbsp;''color(srgb-linear 1 0 0)''
		are [=equivalent colors=].
		They are in different <<color-space>>s
		and neither has a [=missing component=],
		so step 4 converts both to ''oklab'',
		where they have the same value.
	</div>

	<div class="example" id="ex-equivalent-oklab-epsilon">
		<span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(100% 100% 100%)"></span>&nbsp;''color(display-p3 1 1 1)''
		and <span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(100% 100% 100%)"></span>&nbsp;''white''
		are [=equivalent colors=].
		Converting each to ''oklab'' at step 4
		leaves component differences of the order of 10<sup>-16</sup>,
		which is why that step compares with an ε
		rather than for exact equality.
	</div>

	<div class="example" id="ex-equivalent-different-lightness">
		<span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(46.6327% 46.6327% 46.6327%)"></span>&nbsp;''lab(50% 0 0)''
		and <span class="swatch" style="--color: rgb(38.8573% 38.8573% 38.8573%)"></span>&nbsp;''oklab(0.5 0 0)''
		are <em>not</em> [=equivalent colors=].
		Both are achromatic with a lightness of 50%,
		but the two lightness scales are not the same:
		converted to ''oklab'' at step 4
		their lightness components are 0.56897 and 0.5,
		which differ by far more than the standardized ε.
	</div>
